=== Completely New FBMs ===
<span class="mw-customtoggle-newfbms" style="color:blue"><u>'''Click here to expand'''</u></span>
<div class="mw-collapsible mw-collapsed" id="mw-customcollapsible-newfbms">

''' Preparation '''

[https://drive.google.com/file/d/1b6W1BOS90hu6Q90ehBCPGzH4LVVTVCJG/view?usp=sharing PES19 FBM Base Model Pack] - The medical staff model and a couple default textures you'll need during this part

''' Tutorial '''

'''[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Sw_M3oSY7w Video Tutorial]'''

* Make sure you grab the '''Base Model Pack''' as you'll need the medic FMDL bundled in there
* Open two fresh blender windows
* Import '''medical_staff_boots_reweighted.fmdl''' from the '''shaded''' folder into one, have your own model in the other. The contents of the '''shadeless''' folder will be relevant in the video tutorial soontm and have to do with model shading which is a topic separate from the scope of this text tutorial
* Copy the medic suit to the same blender window as your model
* Scale and position your model to match the imported medic model
* Once you're happy with the size and position with your model you might notice the pose is different compared to the medical staff. This is one of the main time sinks when doing FBMs as your model has to be bent into the PES A-pose to work in-game without any clever weight painting tricks. There's two ways to go about this:
** If your model came with a skeleton, select your model in '''Object Mode''', go to the '''Modifiers''' tab, add an '''Armature''' and in the '''Object''' box select your model's skeleton. Now you can select the skeleton itself in '''Object Mode''' and swap to '''Pose Mode''' to easily pose your model using the skeleton it came with
** If your model didn't come with a skeleton, you're going to have to manually deform the model to fit the PES A-pose. This process is really best explained on video, but the basic approach is selecting a section that is not in the right pose (arm, leg most of the time), rotating and grabbing that selection until the selected part is a bit more in the correct shape and slowly working down into a smaller and smaller selection until your entire original selection matches the pose of the medical staff counterpart, Again, better explained on video soontm
* Once you're done scaling/positioning/posing, take another look at your model and make extra sure you've done all you can to make it align with the medical staff. Any extra effort put into this part will save you trouble in the weight painting section
* Once you're happy with the size, scale and pose of your model, select it in '''Object Mode''', go to the '''Modifiers''' tab and select '''Apply'''
* Swap to the '''Data''' tab and and next to the list of '''Vertex Groups''' click the black downwards triangle and select '''Delete All Groups'''
* Select the medical staff model, then with '''Shift''' held select your model and swap to '''Weight Paint''' mode
* Press '''T''' to bring out the left side panel, click '''Transfer Weights''', scroll down to the bottom of the subsection that pops up and change '''Source''' to '''By Name'''
* Blender has now done its best to copy the medical staff's weight painting and apply it to your model. This is why scale, positioning and pose are so important. The closer you can get your source model to the medical staff in all three aspects, the better the resulting transferred weight paint data
* Even with a perfect match between medic and source, there's likely going to be a couple parts you have to adjust by hand. Always give your model a once-over in '''Weight Paint''' mode by scrolling through the list of vertex groups and making sure nothing looks too far out of place. Here are some common weight transfer hiccups to look for:
** The medical staff doesn't have a head, so your model's head will be painted to a combination of head, neck and maybe chest. Fix this by selecting the entire head in '''Edit Mode''', selecting '''sk_head''' in the list of vertex groups and clicking the '''Assign''' button. As always with weight painting, remember to swap to '''Weight Paint''' mode and click '''Normalize All''' in the left side panel any time you do any modifications to the paint
** The medical staff doesn't have hands either. Custom hands with proper movement would make this tutorial too long so they're going to be a separate section. For this tutorial, the best that can be done about hands is selecting them completely in '''Edit Mode''' and assigning them to their respective vertex groups ('''sk_hand_l''', '''sk_hand_r''')
** If your model has some loose bits of clothing, there's a chance parts of it are going to be painted to completely incorrect bones. This often happens with wide or droopy sleeves and wide skirts in general. If a sleeve is painted partially to a thigh or a skirt to an arm, you'll need to manually fix the paint. You can either:
*** Select the part you want to fix in '''Edit Mode''', select the vertex group it's incorrectly painted to, click '''Remove''' and then go to '''Normalize All'''
*** Change to '''Weight Paint''' mode, set the '''Blend''' to '''Substract''' and paint the incorrect groups away, again using '''Normalize All''' after each change
* Once you're happy with the weight paint and have confirmed there's nothing obviously wrong with any of the vertex groups and their paint levels, swap over to the second blender window with the medic model in it and select it
* Switch to '''Edit Mode''', select the entirety of the medic model with '''A''' and drag it to the side
* Swap back to your primary window, select your model and copy it over to the second blender window
* In '''Object Mode''' first select your model, then with '''Shift''' held select the medic and press '''CTRL+J''' to join the two together
* Swap back to '''Edit Mode''', press '''A''' until everything is de-selected and then select the entire medic model and delete it. Make sure you get every part of it to avoid stray bits floating off to the side of your actual model in-game
* Thanks to this maneuver your model has inherited all the properties of the medic model. This is a nice base setup and honestly everything you need in most cases. Some things you might want to change are listed in a separate section at the bottom of this page
* Finally swap back to the '''Scene''' tab, select a location to export to in the plugin's panel, change the filename to be '''boots.fmdl''' and click '''Export FMDL'''.
* Your model should successfully export. The main texture the model uses is called '''body.dds''' and a reference texture you can overwrite with yours is included in the '''Base Model Pack'''. There are some useful settings like transparency and backface culling you might want to check out detailed at the bottom of this page in a separate section.
* Once your model if exported and your texture is done you can [[AET|test it in PES]] and hope it got over in one piece. Good luck
</div>

=== Custom Hand Models ===
<span class="mw-customtoggle-hands" style="color:blue"><u>'''Click here to expand'''</u></span>
<div class="mw-collapsible mw-collapsed" id="mw-customcollapsible-hands">

''' Preparation '''

[https://drive.google.com/file/d/1b6W1BOS90hu6Q90ehBCPGzH4LVVTVCJG/view?usp=sharing Template Pack] - The FBM template now has files for hands as well

''' Tutorial '''

'''[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LrCnQJNsUDc Video Tutorial]'''

First of all, when working on posing your FBM to match the medical staff you should also copy both glove FMDLs from the template pack to your working window and try to line up your model's hands with the PES ones. There's a good chance you'll have to stretch out the arm and make your model's hands bigger to achieve this but some of the damage can be fixed later in PES with physique sliders. Just like how the rest of your model should be decently lined up with the medical staff to make weight painting that much easier and to enable it to move correctly in PES, lining up the hands is even more important because the hand has so many moving parts crammed into such a small space. The step-by-step part will focus on what to do with your custom hands after you're done posing your model.

* Swap to '''Edit Mode''', select your model's hands and separate them into their own mesh with '''P -> Selection'''
* Swap back to '''Object Mode''', select the mesh that now only has your hands, go back to '''Edit Mode''', select only one of the hands this time and separate it further into its own mesh with '''P -> Selection'''
* Swap back to '''Object Mode''', select only one of your hand meshes and import the corresponding glove file from the template pack into another blender window
* Copy your hand into the secondary blender window, make sure you have it selected, go to the '''Data''' tab, click the black downwards triangle to the right of the list of vertex groups and select '''Delete All Groups'''
* Swap to the '''Material''' tab and keep pressing the '''-''' button to the right of the list of materials until all of the materials have been deleted
* Select your custom hand model, then with '''Shift''' held select the template hand, swap to '''Weight Paint''' mode, press '''T''' to bring out the left sidebar and press '''Transfer Weights'''
* Change back to '''Object Mode''', select the template hand, change to '''Edit Mode''', select everything with '''A''' and drag the hand to the side so that it's out of the way for the next steps
* Change back to '''Object Mode''', select your custom hand and change to '''Weight Paint''' mode
* Swap to the '''Data''' tab and select the first vertex group in the list to have the '''skh_''' prefix
* For each vertex group take a close look at your mesh and where the weight paint is. If you notice the paint bleeding over to the neighbouring fingers set your weight painting '''Blend''' mode to '''Subtract''' and erase the paint from the fingers it doesn't belong to. '''DO NOT ''Normalize All'' after each modification''', this is one of the few scenarios in the PES workflow where it's better to wait before normalizing
* Work your way down the list of vertex groups until you've fixed the paint bleeding on all of them. Once you're done with the last group select '''sk_forearm_r/sk_forearm_l''' in the list of vertex groups and click '''Normalize All'''
* Change to '''Edit Mode''', make sure nothing is selected, press '''Spacebar''' and type in '''Select Underweighted Vertices'''. If no vertices got selected you can move on to the next step. The vertices the tool selected have 0 weight and blender wasn't able to normalize them so we have to paint them manually. Scroll through the list of vertex groups in '''Weight Paint''' mode until you find the one they should belong to and paint them to it. You might have multiple 0-weights and they might be in different fingers, make sure to fix all of them accordingly
* Change back to '''Object Mode''', select your mesh and then with '''Shift''' held select the template hand you moved to the side previously and press '''Ctrl+J''' to join the meshes together
* Change to '''Edit Mode''', select the template hand without having any of your custom hand selected and delete it with '''X -> Vertices'''
* The hand is now ready to be exported. If you want you can change the PES-related settings under the '''Material''' and '''Texture''' tabs
* Repeat the same steps for the other hand until done
</div>

== Cardhead ==
[[File:Cardhead_example.png|x500px|frameless|right]]
A cardhead replaces a player's head with a simple textured plane. You can use a [[Aesthetic_Templates|template]] where you only have to replace a texture, but making a cardhead yourself is faster once you know your way around Blender, and it's a decent way to learn some of the basics.

# '''Add''' a '''plane mesh'''.
# '''Rotate''' the plane so that it faces forward, '''move''' it to where the player's head would be, and '''scale''' it to the size you want.
:* Enable the [[Blender basics#Face Orientation|face orientation overlay]] to make sure you rotated it the right way.
# <li value="3">'''Parent ''(Armature Deform)''''' the plane to the PES skeleton.
# In the '''data tab''', add a '''vertex group''' and name it '''''sk_head'''''. Select all vertices and '''assign''' them to the vertex group with a '''weight of 1'''. This will make the vertices move with the ''sk_head'' bone.
[[File:Cardhead_1.png|x400px|thumb|none]]
The model is now ready for '''export as FBX to Blender 2.79''' if you want the simplest form of cardhead where the backside is mirrored ('''use a two-sided material''').
* There's no need to unwrap the plane as primitives in modern Blender come with basic UVs by default.

[[Blender basics#Adding textures|Preview your texture]] on your model to make sure everything looks correct.

=== Non-mirrored backside ===
If you want both sides to look the same instead of the back being mirrored, for example if you have text that you want to be the right way from both sides:
# Go into '''edit mode'''.
# '''Duplicate''' everything; right click or press Enter without moving your cursor to leave it in place.
# With the duplicate geometry still selected, '''rotate 180 degrees''' on the '''Z axis.
:* You'll notice that the two faces will start '''Z-fighting'''. This won't be an issue in PES with a one-sided material, but if you want to fix the issue in Blender, go to the '''material tab''' with your material selected and '''search''' at the top for '''"backface,"''' and then enable '''''Backface Culling'''''.
'''Use a one-sided material'''.

=== Different backside ===
If you want to use a different texture for the back:
# Follow the steps in the section above.
# Select the backwards-facing face and '''separate''' it into its own mesh.
# In Blender 2.79, give the backside mesh a different material.

== Boxhead ==
[[File:Boxhead_example.png|x500px|frameless|right]]
A boxhead replaces a player's head with a simple textured cube. You can use a [[Aesthetic_Templates|template]] where you only have to replace a texture, but making a boxhead yourself is a decent way to learn some of the basics, particularly UV mapping.

#'''Add''' a '''cube mesh'''.
# '''Move''' the cube to where the player's head would be and '''scale''' it to the size you want.
# '''Parent ''(Armature Deform)''''' the cube to the PES skeleton.
# In the '''data tab''', add a '''vertex group''' and name it '''''sk_head'''''. Select all vertices and '''assign''' them to the vertex group with a '''weight of 1'''. This will make the vertices move with the ''sk_head'' bone.
[[File:Boxhead_1.png|x400px|thumb|none]]
#<li value="5"> Next, split your viewport into a '''new window''' and make it a '''UV editor'''. You'll see that the cube has a default cross-shaped UV map. You can use this UV map as-is if you want to; to export the UV map as a guide for making your texture, click '''''UV''''' at the top of the UV editor and then select '''''Export UV Layout'''''.

[[Blender basics#Adding textures|Preview your texture]] on your model to see which face corresponds to which part of the UV map.

The model is now ready for '''export as FBX to Blender 2.79''' if you're content with the default UVs.

You'll notice that each face only occupies 1/16th of the UV area with the default UV map. You can unwrap it yourself so that each face takes up 1/9th of the area:
# '''Select''' everything in '''edit mode'''.
# Open the '''edge tools''' menu and choose '''''Mark Seam'''''. This can be done in either the 3D viewport or the UV editor. All edges should now turn red.
# Select everything and '''unwrap''' the model; choose '''''Unwrap Angle Based'''''.
:* Some faces may end up upside down on the UV map. Add a texture to check and rotate the upside down faces 180 degrees in the UV editor.
[[File:Boxhead_2.png|x300px|thumb|none]]

=== Same texture for all sides ===
If you want all sides of your boxhead to be identical:
# '''Select''' everything in '''edit mode'''.
# '''Unwrap''' and choose '''''Reset'''''. This will make each face take up the entire UV space.
# Fix the '''rotation''' of the faces by selecting them in the 3D viewport and rotating them in the UV editor.

== Head from existing model ==
[[File:Basic_head_example.png|x500px|frameless|right]]
This tutorial will show you how to take a pre-existing model and turn it into a basic head for a PES player.
<br> The process can vary a bit depending on the source of the model. This example is a fairly basic MMD model.

<br> Start by [[Blender basics#Import/Export|importing]] your model. Set your [[Blender basics#Shading modes|shading mode]] to ''Material Preview'' to see the model's textures.

=== Removing the existing skeleton ===
If the model came with a skeleton, you'll want to remove it first.
#'''Select the mesh(es)''' and '''unparent ''(Clear and Keep Transformation)'''''.
:* You sometimes want to pose a model before applying the pose and unparenting, but this tutorial assumes the model needs no posing.
#<li value="2"> '''Delete''' the skeleton. If the skeleton is parented to an [[Blender basics#Empty|empty]], right-click the empty in the outliner and click '''''Delete Hierarchy'''''.
[[File:Blender_head_unparent.mkv.webm|600px|thumb|none|The first few seconds show what will likely happen if you delete a skeleton without unparenting the mesh first.]]

=== Deleting unwanted parts ===
Next, we want to remove all parts of the mesh we don't need.
# '''[[Blender basics#Selection|Select]]''' all parts of the mesh you want to delete. The best way to do it depends on the model.
# '''Delete''' the selected geometry.
:* If you want to close a neck hole, '''delete''' any excess neck, then '''loop select''' the rim of the hole and '''merge ''(At Center)''''', and then move the merged vertex up.
[[File:Blender_head_delete.mkv.webm|600px|thumb|none]]
Depending on the model, you might also want to:
* [[Blender basics#Merge by Distance|Merge by Distance]] if your model had [[Blender basics#Sharp edges|sharp edges]] that got turned into '''split edges''' during the ripping/converting process.
* [[Blender basics#Decimate|Decimate]] the model to simplify it if it has an excessively '''high polycount'''.
* Enable the [[Blender basics#Face Orientation|face orientation overlay]] to check if the model is '''inside-out''' as sometimes happens with ripped models. If the model appears all red, '''flip the normals (Alt+N)'''.

=== Weighting ===
In order for the head to move in-game, it needs to be weighted to the PES skeleton.
<br> This tutorial assumes that you simply want the entire model to move as one solid piece.

# First, you'll need the '''PES skeleton''' from a [[Aesthetic Templates|template]]. You can use either the body or the head skeleton.
# '''Parent ''(Armature Deform)''''' the mesh to the PES skeleton.
# With the mesh selected, go to the '''[[Blender basics#General UI|data tab]]''' of the properties panel. There, you'll see the '''[[Blender basics#Vertex groups|vertex groups]]''' section at the top.
:* If the mesh already has vertex groups, delete them by clicking the chevron and selecting '''''Delete All Groups'''''.
#<li value="4"> We want the whole model to move with the ''sk_head'' bone. To do that, first '''add a new group''' and name it '''''sk_head'''''.
# '''Select everything''' in '''edit mode''' and click '''''Assign''''' to assign them to the selected group. Make sure the '''weight''' slider below is at '''1.000''' before assigning.
[[File:Blender_head_weight.png|x400px|thumb|none]]

=== Positioning ===
To position the mesh so that it lines up with the PES body:
# Import a [[Aesthetic Templates|template]] or a kit model for your team.
# '''Move''' the mesh to the reference mesh's neck and '''scale''' it to the size you want.
:* Since you're only making a head model, either delete the reference mesh once you're done or be mindful not to include it when exporting.

=== Materials & textures ===
When using models from other sources, you often have to clean up the [[Blender basics#Materials|materials]] to make it work in PES and to optimize the model.
# Start by using '''''Remove Unused Material Slots''''' on the mesh(es) to remove any materials that are no longer used after deleting geometry earlier.
# If your model consists of one mesh with multiple materials, select everything in edit mode and '''separate by material''' to make things easier to manage.
# Go through all meshes, delete any you don't want, and '''join''' all meshes that should be '''shaded the same way''' and use the '''same texture''', then remove all materials but one from the merged mesh. To see what texture a material uses, go to the ''Surface'' section at the top of the materials tab and click the arrows next to the parameters to reveal the texture names and options. The name of the parameter you're looking for can vary with the model source.
:* You can generally ignore everything but base color textures for PES modding purposes.
:* Rename your meshes afterwards to make things easier to keep track of.
#<li value="4"> If the model uses a lot of textures (≳4), you should make a [[Blender basics#Texture atlas|texture atlas]]. Repeat step 3 afterwards.
[[File:Blender_head_materials.mkv.webm|600px|thumb|none]]


'''Your model is now ready to be [[Getting a player into PES|put in PES]].'''
<br> [[Blender basics#Export|'''Export''']] the model as FBX and import it in Blender 2.79.

== Outlines ==
[[File:Outline_example_1.png|x500px|thumb|right|Basic outline]]
[[File:Outline_example_2.png|x300px|thumb|right|Colored outlines]]
Outlines for 3D models typically use the inverse shell method, which involves a slightly thicker duplicate of the mesh with inverted normals.

Ideally, you want to do it dynamically using a material, but that's not an option here.


#First, enable the [[Blender basics#Face Orientation|face orientation overlay]] to easily tell if you're doing things correctly.
# If you want to '''exclude''' certain parts of the mesh from the outline, create a '''new [[Blender basics#Vertex groups|vertex group]]''' (name it whatever you want that's not the name of a bone) and '''assign''' all vertices you want to exclude to it. This will be used later.
:* You can also use this to make certain parts of the outline thinner by assigning a weight less than 1.
#<li value="3">'''Duplicate the mesh''' you want to add outlines to.
#'''''[[Blender basics#Mergy by Distance|Merge by Distance]]''''' to remove gaps.
#'''Flip the normals'''. The duplicated mesh should be all red.
[[File:Outlines_2.jpg|x300px|thumb|none]]
#<li value="6"> To '''add thickness''' to the outline, add a '''''Solidify'' modifier''' with the settings below. Set the thickness to your liking (should be negative).
::If you created a '''vertex group''' in '''step 2''', set it as the ''Vertex Group'' in the modifier and enable '''''Vertex Group Invert''''' (double arrow icon) on the right. The modifier will then exclude that vertex group.
:* Make sure to '''apply scale'''. If the mesh's scale isn't 1, the thickness will be wrong.
:* You may also want to play with the '''''Thickness Clamp''''' settings of the modifier depending on your model.

The model is now '''ready for export'''. You don't need to apply the modifier first.
[[File:Outlines_3.jpg|x300px|thumb|none]]
If you want to add outlines to '''multiple meshes''', you can simply copy the solidify modifier to the other outline meshes by clicking the chevron at the top and then clicking '''''Copy to Selected''''' with the original as the active object.

=== Materials & textures ===
* '''Outline materials''' should be '''one-sided''' and '''unlit''' (shadeless).
* Single-color outlines can have their UVs shrunk to occupy just a few pixels, or have a separate texture that's as small as 4x4 pixels.
* Pure black outlines tend to look bad and cause very noticeable aliasing; adding a bit of color makes a big difference.
* For the best results, you want your outline to use the '''same texture as the mesh, but darker'''. Due to being restricted to PES materials, you have to do it manually by duplicating and editing the texture.

